1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A change to a medical-device software
requirement can affect linked hazards,

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
work items, test evidence,
and approval records.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
MedTrace makes those trace relationships
inspectable on live Jira and Confluence data.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
It supports traceability work;
it does not determine regulatory compliance.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
This is a real Jira project and a real
Confluence space — the specifications

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
are synthetic, but they are
live tenant artifacts.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
MedTrace opens empty: LIVE mode starts
fail-closed with zero hazards.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
One click on Sync Live Atlassian
ingests the project's labelled issues.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Four tracked tasks link to their SRS pages
through Jira remote links.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
MEDTRACE-5 is safety-labelled but has no
linked specification — flagged as a gap.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
MEDTRACE-6 is untagged and skipped.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Every test starts UNTESTED and unsigned,
so the release is BLOCKED.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Closing the loop on MEDTRACE-1:
record a PASSED verification result

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
with a real evidence URL,

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
then execute the 21 CFR Part 11 sign-off —

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
an attested electronic signature
over the spec's content hash.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The trace link turns verified.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Now the requirement drifts: in Confluence,

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
SRS-201 changes from 5.0 to 5.5
millilitres per hour.

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
The page-update trigger fires,
the approval signature is revoked,

21
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and one click shows the transitive
blast radius — the spec, its hazard,

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
the Jira task, the test,
and the re-audit checklist.

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The Test Human Gate button invokes
the same validator handler a Jira

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
workflow calls on transition.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,000
With a Class C hazard unverified,
the transition is blocked — fail-closed.

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
A site admin can attach this validator
to a live workflow.

27
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The MedTrace Auditor runs the same
deterministic engine inside Rovo chat.

28
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Asked about MEDTRACE-1, it calls
the hazard-lineage action and returns

29
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
the trace chain and the release verdict —
under the invoking user's own permissions.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The same two actions are exposed
through the Atlassian MCP server at

31
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
mcp.atlassian.com — a site admin enables
external MCP exposure under Connected apps,

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
and the client connects over OAuth.

33
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Here verify-iec-compliance answers
the bolus-rate diff: release blocked.

34
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Evidence exports and the hash-chained
audit trail verify independently.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Evaluators with no tenant data can seed
a labelled DEMO dataset instead.

36
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
MedTrace is assistive traceability tooling —
not a regulatory determination,

37
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
not a validated QMS,
and not for patient data.

38
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
MedTrace — deterministic blast radius,
a hard safety gate,

39
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
and cryptographic evidence,

40
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
on live Atlassian data. Built on Forge.
